\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related_work}

\paragraph{Temporal abstraction in world-model RL.}
The options framework \citep{sutton1999options} and action-repetition methods \citep{sharma2017figar, biedenkapp2021temporl} achieve temporal abstraction via real transitions. The Dreamer family \citep{hafner2020dreamerv1, hafner2021dreamerv2, hafner2023dreamerv3, hafner2025dreamerv4} trains policies in imagination at fixed $\dt{=}1$; hierarchical variants \citep{gumbsch2024thick, mattes2024hieros, li2025lsimagine} maintain separate dynamics per scale. TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} conditions a world model on $\dt$ within TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} and achieves consistent gains; the critical distinction is that MPC re-plans from real states, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic (\S\ref{sec:discussion}).

\paragraph{Value overestimation and model fidelity.}
Maximization bias \citep{thrun1993issues} has been addressed by Double Q-learning \citep{vanhasselt2016double}, TD3 \citep{fujimoto2018td3}, and ensemble methods \citep{chen2021redq}; in model-based settings, MBPO \citep{janner2019mbpo} and MOPO \citep{yu2020mopo} bound rollout error. \citet{lu2018nondelusional} identify delusional bias from policy-class constraints. CIVO is mechanistically distinct: the amplifier is the world model's co-adaptation with a learned temporal embedding, producing systematic reward-prediction bias at $\dt > 1$. Compounding error in multi-step rollouts \citep{lambert2020objective, asadi2019combating} exacerbates this bias; our gradient decomposition confirms the observation decoder channel as the carrier of $\dt$-specific information. Our factorial diagnostic follows \citet{henderson2018deep} in systematically isolating design-choice effects.

