\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping}
\label{sec:sgs_method}

To identify which gradient pathway through $e_{\Delta t}$ drives CIVO, we introduce \textit{selective gradient stopping} (SGS): during the backward pass, $e_{\Delta t}$ is detached before entering selected prediction heads via the stop-gradient operator $\operatorname{sg}(\cdot)$, preventing those loss gradients from updating $e_{\Delta t}$'s parameters while preserving its forward-pass contribution.

We test three variants. \textbf{Reward-only SGS} (exp\_016e) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from the reward head only: $\hat{r}_t = f_{\text{reward}}(\operatorname{sg}(e_{\Delta t}), h_t, z_t)$, testing whether the reward pathway drives CIVO. \textbf{Full-pathway SGS} (exp\_018) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from all non-KL gradient pathways (observation decoders, reward head, and continuation predictor), retaining only the KL dynamics loss signal. \textbf{Obs-decoder-only SGS} (exp\_025/026) detaches $e_{\Delta t}$ from the observation decoder heads only, preserving reward and KL gradient signals. This variant tests whether targeting the identified causal pathway suffices: gradient decomposition identifies the observation decoder as the sole carrier of $\dt$-specific spatial information (16--22\% of gradient magnitude), while the reward head (38--49\%) does not encode $\dt$-specific information despite its larger magnitude. To quantify each pathway's contribution, we perform gradient decomposition using \texttt{torch.autograd.grad}, measuring the $\ell_2$ norm of $\nabla_\theta \mathcal{L}_i$ for each loss component $\mathcal{L}_i$, where $\theta$ denotes the temporal embedding parameters.

\subsection{Forward-Pass Interventions}
\label{sec:forward_pass_method}

The SGS results establish that gradient-level interventions cannot eliminate CIVO (\S\ref{sec:f6}). We test two forward-pass interventions as representation- and output-level probes of CIVO's vulnerability surface.

\paragraph{Temporal Conditioning Noise (TCN).}
During imagination, Gaussian noise $\mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$ is added to the temporal embedding output before it enters the RSSM, where $\sigma$ is specified as a fraction of the embedding's running standard deviation. By degrading the precision of $\dt$-conditioning at the representation level, TCN tests whether reducing the critic's ability to exploit precise but inaccurate temporal predictions suffices to suppress CIVO. We test $\sigma \in \{0.5, 0.2\}$ across Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, and PickCube-v1.

\paragraph{Reality-Anchored Value Estimation (RAVE).}
An exponential moving average (EMA) tracks the critic's value estimates on real environment states. During imagination, any value estimate exceeding the EMA anchor by more than a ceiling factor is clipped: $V_{\text{clipped}} = \min(V_{\text{imag}}, \text{EMA} \times c)$. This directly limits critic overestimation without affecting the world model's representation. We test $c = 2.0$ and $c = 1.5$.

