\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

Full mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.

\paragraph{CIVO mechanism and gradient analysis.}
The RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\times$ prediction gap between $\dt{=}1$ and $\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (Table~\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}); the critic internalizes this bias and the actor exploits it, creating a four-stage feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced. Gradient decomposition (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}) reveals that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes 38--49\% of $\nabla_\theta e_{\dt}$ yet reward-only SGS fails catastrophically (NVE $10.42\times$), while the observation decoder (16--22\%) carries the critical $\dt$-specific spatial predictions. All three SGS variants exhibit compensatory amplification---blocking any pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones---and full-pathway SGS (0/3 elimination) establishes forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability floor. Additionally, obs-decoder SGS trades overestimation for systematic underestimation: all four Walker seeds reach terminal $v/e$ ratios of $0.056$--$0.14$ by 215K, likely because detaching observation decoder gradients damages the shared RSSM latent space (Appendix~\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).

\paragraph{MPC vs.\ imagination-based actor-critic.}
TAWM \citep{nhu2025tawm} succeeds with $\dt$-conditioning in TD-MPC2 \citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} because MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic. In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination: the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\dt > 1$ produces biased value estimates that the actor exploits, closing a positive feedback loop with no MPC counterpart (Appendix~\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).

\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}
The intervention hierarchy (Table~\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}) yields actionable recommendations: (1)~monitor for CIVO using the NVE diagnostic ($\geq 1.5\times$, AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\% CI: 0.904--1.000, $n{=}29$; Appendix~\ref{sec:roc_analysis}); the Youden-optimal threshold $\theta^* = 1.53$ coincides with the preset $1.5\times$ cutoff, confirming this choice is statistically principled; (2)~no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments---TCN ($\sigma{=}0.2$) prevents CIVO on Walker (2/2 seeds) but fails on Cheetah and PickCube, and even successful prevention exhibits high eval variance (45--91\%); (3)~avoid gradient-level interventions (compensatory amplification) and output-level value capping (anchor pollution).

