\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We present a factorial diagnostic of temporal abstraction in DreamerV3, identifying trained $\dt$-embedding combined with multi-rate data exposure as the causal factor behind CIVO ($\nve \geq 1.5\times$ threshold, 27/29 accuracy, AUROC${=}0.971$). The RSSM develops a $2.78\times$ reward prediction gap that drives critic overestimation; no gradient-level intervention eliminates CIVO (full-pathway SGS 0/3, obs-decoder SGS 1/6), confirming forward-pass representation-level mechanisms. A three-level intervention hierarchy reveals that even representation-level TCN ($\sigma{=}0.2$) achieves only environment-specific partial mitigation (Walker 2/2 prevented, eval 45--91\%; Cheetah and PickCube 0/2). The contrast with TAWM identifies the feedback architecture as the structural factor: $\dt$-conditioning succeeds with MPC re-planning but fails under imagination-based bootstrapping.

\paragraph{Limitations.}
Our analysis focuses on Walker Walk; cross-environment evidence uses fewer seeds. Both environments use DreamerV3/RSSM; generalization to transformer-based world models remains open. TCN seed~1 exhibits performance degradation after its peak (401 $\to$ 161 by 215K), unlike baseline slow seeds that converge, suggesting noise injection may impair late-stage policy refinement. The NVE threshold produces two non-CIVO false positives (27/29).

\paragraph{Future directions.}
Hierarchical temporal abstraction may avoid flat-conditioning failure by maintaining separate dynamics per scale. Developing interventions that account for environment-specific CIVO severity remains an open challenge. Code and training logs will be released upon acceptance.

